{
  "fromId": "j_affricate",
  "toId": "dz",
  "preamble": "Fronting of the voiced postalveolar affricate [dʒ] to the alveolar affricate [dz]. Nepali, Marathi and Konkani have [dz] where Hindi has [dʒ] (Hindi आज [ɑːd͡ʒ] 'today' : Nepali [ä.d͡zʌ], Marathi /ad͡z/); in Marathi and Konkani the dental affricate is the reflex before non-front vowels. The same substitution occurs in loanword adaptation: Modern Greek renders Turkish c [dʒ] as ⟨τζ⟩ /dz/ (τζαμί 'mosque', τζάμι 'glass'), and Sardinian adapts Italian [dʒ] as [dz]. Polish mazurzenie (dż [d͡ʐ] > dz) is filed under the retroflex cell ɖʐ → dz, not here.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Depalatalization, Fronting",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Nepali",
      "languageFamily": "Indo-Aryan",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Hindi आज /ɑːd͡ʒ/ 'today' (cognate retaining [dʒ])",
          "to": "आज [ä.d͡zʌ]",
          "note": "Nepali ज is the alveolar affricate /d͡z/; both words are inherited from Prakrit."
        },
        {
          "from": "Hindi जौ /d͡ʒɔː/ 'barley'",
          "to": "जौ /d͡zʌu̯/",
          "note": "Inherited from Prakrit java (Sanskrit yava); Nepali has /dz/ where Hindi has /dʒ/."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Marathi",
      "languageFamily": "Indo-Aryan",
